/* Dom Quixote de La Mancha — Parte 7 — English (British)
   Literary translation for children aged 5–8, from the Brazilian Portuguese adaptation.
   Names as in Parts 1–6 and in the bookshelf (basin-helmet, the steward, Master Nicolás,
   Sansón Carrasco, Barataria, Doctor Pedro Recio). The doctor's village, "Tirafora" in Portuguese
   (Cervantes' Tirteafuera, "take yourself off"), is "Takeaway": a doctor who takes the food away.
   The activities keep the Portuguese ids (the tiny caps, the cane and the farewell scenes are
   switched on by id); only the words and the trick words (CAKE, CAGE) are English.
   Check with: npm run conferir:traducoes */
(window.TRADUCOES = window.TRADUCOES || {})['en'] = {
    obra: 'Don Quixote of La Mancha',
    paginas: [
        {   // página 0 (capa)
            hash: 'b8c9460675',
            titulo: 'Part 7: Sancho, Governor of the Island',
            texto: `Sancho Panza has been waiting for this day ever since his very first adventure: he is going to govern an {{ISLAND|IS • LAND|🏝️}}! But it is a very strange island... It has no beach, it has no fish and, however hard Sancho looks, it has no sea.

In this part of the story, the new governor will solve detective cases, stand up to the most annoying doctor in the world and find out who owns that deep voice. Meanwhile, at the palace, Don Quixote will feel something new: {{LONGING|LONG • ING|💭}} for his squire.

Turn the page and find out: can a simple man really {{GOVERN|GOV • ERN|⚖️}}?`
        },
        {   // página 1
            hash: '9310fb2dfe',
            capitulo: '💡 Chapter 52 • Advice for a Governor',
            titulo: 'Enough Sayings, Sancho!',
            texto: `On the night before the journey, Don Quixote took Sancho to a stone bench in the garden. He was happy and worried at the same time, like a father on his son’s first day at school. “Sit down, Sancho. A governor needs good {{ADVICE|AD • VICE|💡}}, and I am going to give you the best I have.

“First: never be ashamed of being a farmer’s son. Second: treat the rich and the poor in the same way, and listen to both of them right to the end. Third: when you have to give a punishment, remember that {{JUSTICE|JUS • TICE|⚖️}} is more beautiful when it holds hands with forgiveness.” Sancho listened with his eyes wide open.

“Now, the advice for the body. Cut your nails. Walk slowly. Don’t eat garlic or onions before meetings, so that nobody can smell you from far away. And, for heaven’s sake, Sancho: don’t burp at the table!” “Not even a tiny one?” “Not even a tiny one.”

“And stop this habit of talking in {{SAYINGS|SAY • INGS|💬}}!” “Leave it to me, master. Forewarned is forearmed. A closed mouth catches no flies. And slow and steady wins the race!” “Sancho! That was three at once!” “It’s because they live in my mouth, master, and they all come out together, like hens when you open the henhouse.”

Only one thing still worried the knight: “A governor who can’t read...” Sancho puffed out his chest: “But I already know the little ants of my name! And I’m going to learn to write every one of them.” Don Quixote hugged his friend. On the other side of the garden, the duke’s steward, the tall one with the deep voice, was waiting to take the new governor away. Sancho looked at him and frowned: “Master... listen to that voice! It’s just like the bearded countess’s!” Don Quixote listened: “It is a little like it. But it can’t be: the countess is a countess, and the steward is a steward. Keep your eyes open, Sancho, and tell me afterwards.”`
        },
        {   // página 2
            hash: '923d6bbbe6',
            capitulo: '🧵 Chapter 53 • The Island with No Sea',
            titulo: 'The Case of the Five Tiny Caps',
            texto: `Sancho set off dressed as a governor, with a long coat and a hat with a feather. He rode on a mule, but he made sure that his donkey came right behind, with a new saddle and silk ribbons. Every now and then he turned his head, just to look at his friend, as pleased as could be.

They came to a pretty town with a wall all around it. The bells rang, the people came out into the street, and they handed Sancho the keys to the gate. “Welcome to the Island of {{BARATARIA|BA • RA • TA • RI • A|🏝️}}, my lord governor!” Sancho looked to the right. He looked to the left. He climbed onto a little stool and looked over the wall. “Excuse me... and the sea, where is it?” “The sea has the day off today,” answered the steward, very quickly.

They took the governor to the courtroom and sat him on an enormous chair. The steward, with a little notebook in his hand, wrote everything down. “It is the custom of the island,” he explained. “The new governor solves a few difficult cases, so that the people can see whether he is clever or silly.” Sancho gulped and rolled up his sleeves.

In came a farmer and a {{TAILOR|TAI • LOR|🧵}}, which is the person who makes clothes. “My lord governor,” said the farmer, “I took him a piece of cloth and asked if there was enough to make a cap. He said there was. I thought he wanted to keep the leftovers for himself, so I asked if there was enough for two. There was. For three? There was. For four? There was. For five? There was! Today I went to fetch my five caps...” “And here they are!” said the tailor. He opened his hand: on each {{FINGER|FIN • GER|☝️}} there was a tiny cap, the size of a fingertip.

The whole room burst out laughing. Sancho counted the tiny caps, scratched his beard and decided: “Each of them tried to be cleverer than the other, and both of them came off badly. The farmer loses his cloth, and the tailor loses his payment. And the tiny caps go to the children of the town, who must have dolls just the right size!” The people clapped. The steward wrote in his notebook, amazed: "This governor is nobody’s fool".`,
            cartoes: {
                tipo: 'ordem',
                ordem: ['um', 'dois', 'tres', 'quatro', 'cinco'],
                itens: [
                    { id: 'tres', palavra: 'THREE', emoji: '🎩' },
                    { id: 'cinco', palavra: 'FIVE', emoji: '🎩' },
                    { id: 'um', palavra: 'ONE', emoji: '🎩' },
                    { id: 'quatro', palavra: 'FOUR', emoji: '🎩' },
                    { id: 'dois', palavra: 'TWO', emoji: '🎩' }
                ]
            }
        },
        {   // página 3
            hash: '907b5efba4',
            capitulo: '🍗 Chapter 54 • The Plate-Snatching Doctor',
            titulo: 'The Doctor’s Wand',
            texto: `After so much judging, the governor was as hungry as a lion. They took Sancho to a room with a long table, full of dishes giving off little puffs of steam. He tied his napkin round his neck and rubbed his hands. Beside his chair, standing very straight, was a thin, serious man with a slender {{WAND|WAND|🪄}} in his hand.

They put an enormous cake in front of Sancho, covered in cream and sugar. He had hardly picked up his fork when the wand touched the plate, and a servant carried it away in the blink of an eye. Then came a dish of crackling, dripping with fat. The wand touched it: gone. Then came a roast chicken, golden brown. The wand touched it: gone as well. Sancho watched the dishes arriving and leaving, like someone watching a race.

“What sort of game is this?” “I am the {{DOCTOR|DOC • TOR|🩺}} of the island, my lord governor. My job is to look after your health. So much sugar makes your teeth cry. So much fat makes your tummy heavy.” “All right. And the chicken?” “The chicken... has wings. It might want to fly out of your tummy!” Just then a basket of fruit arrived: apples, pears and grapes. Sancho grabbed an apple very quickly and hid it behind his back. The wand went up... stopped in mid-air... and came down ever so slowly. “Fruit is allowed,” said the doctor. “Fruit is good for you.” “Phew! And what else can I eat?” “Nothing else. Just a few very thin little biscuits.”

Sancho leaned back in his chair and took a good look at the man: “What is your name, doctor?” “Doctor Pedro Recio, born in the village of Takeaway.” “Well then, Doctor Pedro Recio of Takeaway: take yourself away from here this minute! Or else I’ll pick up this chair and... and sit on it very crossly! Sugary cake I’ll keep for party days, but I want bread, onion and a good big piece of {{CHEESE|CHEESE|🧀}}, which is the food I grew up on!”

The doctor tiptoed out, wand and all, and the steward coughed to hide his laughter. And that night, for the first time, the governor had his supper: bread, cheese, onion, a vegetable soup and, for pudding, the apple he had saved from the wand. “Eating well isn’t eating almost nothing,” he said, as happy as could be. “It’s eating a little bit of everything, with fruit and vegetables on your plate. An empty tummy can’t govern anybody!” “Was that a saying?” asked the steward, pencil in hand. “It was. But my master isn’t here to hear it.”`
        },
        {   // página 4
            hash: 'bbb96519cd',
            capitulo: '⚖️ Chapter 55 • The Crafty Old Man',
            titulo: 'The Cleverest Judgement',
            texto: `The next day, two old men came into the courtroom. One of them was leaning on a thick wooden {{CANE|CANE|🦯}}. The other, a villager with his hat in his hand, spoke first: “My lord governor, a long time ago I lent this neighbour of mine ten gold {{COINS|COINS|🪙}}. Now he says he has already paid me back. I don’t remember that, and nobody saw it! I only ask one thing: that he swears it. If he swears, I’ll believe him.”

“I swear!” said the old man with the cane. But first he turned to the villager: “Hold my cane for me here, please, it’s getting in my way.” The villager held the cane with both hands. The old man put his hand on his heart and spoke very loudly: “I swear that I put the ten coins into his hands!” Then he took his cane back, bowed his head and began to walk out, as calm as could be.

The villager sighed: “If he swore it, it must be true. It must be me who is getting forgetful.” Sancho put his finger on his forehead and stayed quiet for a long time, with his eyes closed. The room waited. The steward waited, with his pencil in the air. Suddenly, the governor opened his eyes: “Call that man back!”

“Why would somebody ask another man to hold his cane right at the moment of swearing?” wondered Sancho out loud. “And why take it back so quickly? There’s something fishy here...” The old man went white. The governor held out his hand: “Hand that cane over here!” Then he gave the cane to the villager: “Take this cane home. With it, you are paid!” “Paid?” said the villager in surprise. “With an old cane that isn’t worth a single coin?” “That’s what we are going to see.”

Sancho twisted the handle of the cane and, CLICK!, it opened. Ten gold coins rolled out across the floor! The crafty old man had hidden the money inside his cane and then put the cane into his neighbour’s hands. So, when he swore "I put them into his hands", it wasn’t a whole lie... but it was a whole big trick! The old man gave back the coins and said sorry, red with {{SHAME|SHAME|😳}}. One coin rolled and rolled and went straight into the steward’s shoe. “Is that one a tip?” asked Sancho. The steward gave the coin back, very quickly. And the people left the courtroom saying: “This governor can even see inside a cane!”`,
            cartoes: {
                tipo: 'pedido',
                pedidos: ['bengala'],
                itens: [
                    { id: 'cake', palavra: 'CAKE', emoji: '🎂', pegadinha: "Cake? The doctor’s wand whisked the cake away before anybody could look inside it! Let’s try again!" },
                    { id: 'bengala', palavra: 'CANE', emoji: '❓' },
                    { id: 'cage', palavra: 'CAGE', emoji: '🦁', pegadinha: "Cage? There’s no lion in this courtroom, and no coins in a cage either! Let’s try again!" }
                ]
            }
        },
        {   // página 5
            hash: 'a7e1a20658',
            capitulo: '🌉 Chapter 56 • The Bridge of the Difficult Question',
            titulo: 'To Cross or Not to Cross?',
            texto: `Late in the afternoon, when Sancho was already dreaming of his supper, a dusty traveller came into the courtroom, with a wide hat and a staff in his hand. “My lord governor, I have come from very far away. In my land something has happened that no judge can solve. They told me that whoever finds coins inside a cane will surely solve this one too.”

“Tell me, then, and I’ll listen,” said Sancho. “In my land there is a wide river, and across the river there is a {{BRIDGE|BRIDGE|🌉}}. Right at the entrance, before the bridge begins, there are two guards and a {{JAIL|JAIL|🔒}}. The owner of the bridge made a law: whoever arrives tells the guards where he is going. Whoever tells the truth crosses the bridge. Whoever lies doesn’t cross: he goes straight to jail, right there beside it.” “A good law,” said Sancho. “Nobody lies there.” “Nobody used to lie, sir. One would say "I’m going to the market" and cross. Another would say "I’m going to visit my granny" and cross as well.

“Then, yesterday, a man arrived, and the guards asked him, as always: "Where are you going?". And he answered, very calmly: "I am going to jail". The guards scratched their heads. If they let the man cross the bridge, he wasn’t going to jail: so he had lied... and whoever lies doesn’t cross, he goes to jail! But if they put the man in jail, he really was going where he said: so he had told the truth... and whoever tells the truth has to cross! Lock him up, let him go, lock him up, let him go... The guards walked up and down, thinking, until they felt dizzy.”

The whole courtroom sat with their mouths open. The steward scratched his head with his pencil. Sancho thought and thought, and said: “I’ve got it! What if the man lives right in the middle of the bridge, not on one side and not on the other?” “But, my lord governor, nobody can live on a bridge!” “True... and the bed wouldn’t even fit. I was only thinking out loud.”

Then Sancho opened his arms wide, like the two pans of a pair of {{SCALES|SCALES|⚖️}}: “Look. On this side, there is a reason to lock the man up. On this other side, there is a reason just the same to let him go. The scales have stopped right in the middle. And, when we don’t know whether to punish or to forgive, it is better to forgive. My master taught me that, before I came here: justice is more beautiful when it holds hands with forgiveness. Let the man cross!” The traveller clapped, the people clapped, and the steward wrote in his notebook, in shaky handwriting: "Wiser than many a doctor".`
        },
        {   // página 6
            hash: 'ac18d5613a',
            capitulo: '📿 Chapter 57 • The Coral Necklace',
            titulo: 'A Letter for Teresa',
            texto: `Meanwhile, back in the village in La Mancha, Teresa Panza was washing clothes in the stream, with Sanchica beside her. A well-dressed young man arrived on horseback, a {{PAGE|PAGE|🧑}} from the palace of the duke and duchess: that is what they called a boy who carries messages. He got down, took off his hat and knelt in front of Teresa, who was wearing her apron and had wet feet: “Lady Teresa Panza, wife of the lord governor of the Island of Barataria: I bring a letter and a present!”

Teresa looked behind her, to see if there was another Teresa about. There wasn’t. The page took out of his pocket a coral {{NECKLACE|NECK • LACE|📿}}, made of little red stones that come from the sea, with a gold clasp. It was a present from the duchess. Sanchica gave a shout: “Mum! Dad has got the island!” “Didn’t I tell you?” answered Teresa, who had never said any such thing.

There was only one problem: Teresa couldn’t read either. She ran off down the street, with the necklace round her neck and the letter in her hand, until she found the priest, Master Nicolás and Sansón Carrasco, who were chatting in the square. “I’ll read it, Lady Teresa!” offered the page, running after her. “Certainly not: I want the whole village to hear!” Sansón read it aloud: "My dear little Teresa: I want you to know that I am a governor now. I send one kiss for you and two for Sanchica. The donkey is well and sends his regards. Your husband, Sancho Panza. It was the secretary who wrote this, because the little ants of my name won’t come out of my quill yet". “Quill?” said Sanchica, puzzled. “Has Dad turned into a bird?” Everybody laughed. In those days there were no pens like ours: people wrote with a goose {{QUILL|QUILL|🪶}}, a feather with its tip cut very thin, dipped in a little pot of ink.

The priest and Master Nicolás stood with their mouths open: “Governor? Our Sancho?” “And of an island!” said Teresa. “Sanchica, my girl, from today on we only go about in a carriage!” Sanchica wanted to know: “Mum, can I go in the carriage to fetch water from the fountain?”

Teresa sent the duchess the best present she had: a cheese and a little bag of {{ACORNS|A • CORNS|🌰}}, the little fruits of the oak tree, chosen one by one. Sansón helped to tie up the parcel and started chatting to the page, as if it were nothing at all: “So Master Alonso is at the palace of the duke and duchess... And afterwards, where is he planning to go?” The page told him. Sansón gave that little smile of someone who is keeping a secret. That same afternoon, he went to borrow an old shield and a tin of white paint from Master Nicolás. “What for?” “It’s a secret!”`
        },
        {   // página 7
            hash: '9b66975569',
            capitulo: '✉️ Chapter 58 • Don Quixote Misses His Squire',
            titulo: 'The Governor’s First Signature',
            texto: `At the palace of the duke and duchess, every day was a party. But Don Quixote had gone quiet. He missed having someone to grumble about the sun, the rain and being hungry. He even missed the sayings! One night, alone in his room, he went to take off his stocking and, zip!, it came undone all the way, from top to bottom. The knight sat staring at the hole. He had nobody to show it to.

So he took a quill and paper and wrote a {{LETTER|LET • TER|✉️}}: "Sancho, my friend: they tell me you govern like a grown-up, and I am ever so proud. Be kind to the poor. Visit the market and the bakery. And don’t forget to cut your nails. The palace here is very beautiful, but Rocinante asks about the donkey every day. Your master, Don Quixote".

The letter arrived on the island, and the steward, who was also the governor’s secretary, read it aloud, because Sancho couldn’t read. The governor listened, sniffed and wiped his eyes on his sleeve. “Write down the answer: "My master: governing is a lot of work and very little food. A doctor wouldn’t let me have my supper, but I sent the doctor packing. I have already made a good law: the bread on the island must be big and cheap. The donkey sends a bray to Rocinante: HEE-HAAAW". With three As, steward! Don’t you forget a single one.”

“Now the lord governor must {{SIGN|SIGN|✍️}},” said the steward. Sancho held the writing {{QUILL|QUILL|🪶}} the way you hold a fork. He knew the little ants of his name, from looking at them so often in the book. But writing was another story! Which one came first?`,
            cartoes: {
                tipo: 'ordem',
                ordem: ['s', 'a', 'n', 'c', 'h', 'o'],
                itens: [
                    { id: 'n', palavra: 'N', emoji: '✍️' },
                    { id: 'o', palavra: 'O', emoji: '✍️' },
                    { id: 's', palavra: 'S', emoji: '✍️' },
                    { id: 'h', palavra: 'H', emoji: '✍️' },
                    { id: 'a', palavra: 'A', emoji: '✍️' },
                    { id: 'c', palavra: 'C', emoji: '✍️' }
                ]
            }
        },
        {   // página 8
            hash: '3a2183ef4e',
            capitulo: '🐢 Chapter 59 • The Pretend Attack',
            titulo: 'A Hug for the Donkey',
            texto: `On the seventh night of his government, Sancho was asleep when the whole town woke up shouting. Bells, drums, bugles! “Wake up, my lord governor! Enemies have invaded the island!” Sancho sat up in bed, in his nightcap: “But what enemies? This island hasn’t even got a sea for them to come by!”

Nobody listened. They tied two enormous shields onto him, one in front and one behind, so tightly that only his head, his hands and his feet were left sticking out. “Come on, governor! Lead the way!” Sancho tried to take a step and WHUMP: he fell on his back and lay there rocking, like a {{TORTOISE|TOR • TOISE|🐢}} upside down. In the dark, the shouting rushed from one side to the other: “This way! Shut the gate! Bring the ladders!”

Suddenly, the steward shouted louder than anyone: “Enough! Victory! The enemies have run away! Long live the governor!” And he ran to untie the shields and set Sancho on his feet. Sancho said nothing. He looked slowly at one, then at another... and saw that some of them were hiding their laughter. Not the steward. Then Sancho understood everything: the attack was pretend. And so, it seemed, was the island.

In silence, he put on his old clothes and went to the stable. He hugged his {{DONKEY|DON • KEY|🫏}} round the neck and kissed him on the forehead: “My companion, when I went about with you, my days were happy. All I thought about was mending your saddle and filling your tummy. Since I became a governor, I have had a thousand worries and not one peaceful supper.”

Sancho climbed onto the donkey and spoke to everyone, without any anger at all: “Make way, good sirs. I wasn’t born to be a governor. I arrived without a single coin and I am leaving without a single coin. All I ask for is half a loaf and half a cheese for the journey.” The steward, who was no longer laughing, lowered his eyes: “You have been the best governor this town has ever had.” And he took off his hat. Sancho looked at his feet: they were the very same men’s boots that the bearded countess had worn! “I knew it!” he said, and winked. The steward went red and winked back. Sancho rode away with his head held high, on the way to his {{FREEDOM|FREE • DOM|🕊️}}.`
        },
        {   // página 9
            hash: 'ae54a59490',
            capitulo: '🕳️ Chapter 60 • The Voice in the Hole',
            titulo: 'I Know That Bray!',
            texto: `Sancho wanted to get to the palace quickly, to hug his master. But night fell halfway there, as dark as coal. He left the road to look for a little corner to sleep in and... the ground disappeared! Sancho and the donkey slid down into a deep {{HOLE|HOLE|🕳️}}, in the middle of some ruins. They landed on a pile of dry leaves. They weren’t hurt, but they had no way of getting out either.

“Help!” shouted Sancho all night long. Only the echo answered. He shared the half loaf and the half cheese with the donkey. “Do you see, my friend? Yesterday, a governor, on a velvet chair. Today, at the bottom of a hole.” The donkey chewed and listened, with his ears drooping.

Very early in the morning, Don Quixote went out to practise with Rocinante, as he did every day. Suddenly, Rocinante stopped and sniffed the air: he had smelled his friend the donkey! He walked off on his own to the edge of a hole, and from inside came a weak little voice: “Hey, up there! Is there a kind soul who will help a governor without a government?” Don Quixote opened his eyes wide: “Who is down there?” “Sancho Panza!” “It can’t be! My Sancho is governing an island. You must be a {{GHOST|GHOST|👻}} with his voice!”

“Ghost my foot, master! It’s me, all flesh, bone and hunger!” Just at that moment, the donkey filled his chest and let out a bray so loud that the stones shook: HEE-HAAAW! Don Quixote slapped his forehead: “I know that {{BRAY|BRAY|📢}} as if it were one of the family! It’s the donkey! So it really is you, Sancho! Wait there, I’ll go and fetch help!”

The palace servants came with long ropes and pulled up first the donkey, then the governor. Sancho came out with leaves all over his hair. The two friends looked at each other for a moment and hugged so tightly that the basin-helmet fell to the ground. “I missed you, Sancho,” said Don Quixote. “I tore a stocking and had nobody to tell.” “And I, master, governed a whole island and had nobody to tell a saying to.”`
        },
        {   // página 10
            hash: '6fe7788ef2',
            capitulo: '🕊️ Chapter 61 • Farewell to the Palace',
            titulo: 'Bread with Freedom',
            texto: `At the palace, Sancho knelt before the duke and the duchess, with the keys of the town in his hand: “My lord and lady, I know that island was only make-believe. But the people there were real, and so was the hunger. I governed for ten days: I stole nothing, I gained nothing, I made a few good laws and solved a few difficult cases. Now I give back the {{KEYS|KEYS|🔑}} and go back to being a squire, which is a job where you get to eat.”

The duke and the duchess felt awkward. They had invented the island as a joke, a play, just like the one with the bearded countess and the flying horse. But the steward had shown them his little notebook, with everything written down. “Sancho,” said the duchess, looking down, “the island was make-believe. But your {{WISDOM|WIS • DOM|🦉}} was real.” “Just tell me one thing, my lady: what about the sea? Has it had the day off until today?” “The sea... never existed,” she said, blushing. “It was my fault, Sancho,” said the duke. “Forgive me.” Don Quixote put his hand on his friend’s shoulder: “The island was pretend. But the governor was real.” “You are forgiven,” answered Sancho. “The only one I won’t forgive is Doctor Pedro Recio of Takeaway, who made me have little biscuits for supper!”

Don Quixote wanted to leave as well. The palace was beautiful and had plenty of food, but he missed the open sky. The duke and duchess gave Sancho a little purse of coins for the journey, and the duchess proudly showed everyone the acorns that Teresa had sent. Then the great {{GATE|GATE|🚪}} of the palace creaked open, and the two friends set off along the {{ROAD|ROAD|🛤️}}.

On the road, with the wind on his face, Don Quixote took a deep breath: “Ah, Sancho! {{FREEDOM|FREE • DOM|🕊️}} is one of the most precious gifts that heaven has ever given to people. No treasure on land or in the sea is worth as much as freedom. Back at the palace, in the middle of all those banquets, I felt as squeezed as a foot in a new shoe!”

“That was beautifully said, master. Bread with freedom is good. But with cheese it’s even better.” Don Quixote laughed. “And now, where are we going?” “To {{BARCELONA|BAR • CE • LO • NA|🌊}}, Sancho! They say there are festivals there, knights and something neither of us has ever seen: the sea.” “A real sea? This time I want to see it with my own eyes!” And the two of them went on along the road. Far behind, a long way off, a lone knight was coming along the same road. On his shield shone a white moon.`,
            cartoes: {
                tipo: 'ordem',
                ordem: ['chaves', 'portao', 'estrada'],
                itens: [
                    { id: 'estrada', palavra: 'ROAD', emoji: '🕊️' },
                    { id: 'chaves', palavra: 'KEYS', emoji: '🕊️' },
                    { id: 'portao', palavra: 'GATE', emoji: '🕊️' }
                ]
            }
        }
    ],
    textos: {
        "Dom Quixote e Sancho Pança lado a lado, olhando o caminho que têm pela frente": "Don Quixote and Sancho Panza side by side, looking at the road ahead of them",
        "👓 Lente da Realidade: Dois Amigos e um Longo Caminho": "👓 Reality Lens: Two Friends and a Long Road",
        "✨ Lente da Imaginação: O Cavaleiro e o Governador da Ilha!": "✨ Imagination Lens: The Knight and the Governor of the Island!",
        "Num banco de pedra do jardim, Dom Quixote dá conselhos a Sancho, que escuta com atenção": "On a stone bench in the garden, Don Quixote gives Sancho advice, and Sancho listens carefully",
        "👓 Lente da Realidade: Conselhos de um Amigo": "👓 Reality Lens: Advice from a Friend",
        "✨ Lente da Imaginação: O Futuro Governador da Ilha Barataria!": "✨ Imagination Lens: The Future Governor of the Island of Barataria!",
        "Ajude o governador a conferir os chapeuzinhos! Toque nos números na ordem certa: cada acerto põe um chapeuzinho num dedo do alfaiate.": "Help the governor check the tiny caps! Tap the numbers in the right order: each right answer puts a tiny cap on one of the tailor’s fingers.",
        "No tribunal da vila, Sancho, na cadeira de governador, olha a mão aberta do alfaiate; ao lado, o lavrador de braços cruzados e o mordomo alto com um caderninho": "In the town courtroom, Sancho, in the governor’s chair, looks at the tailor’s open hand; beside them, the farmer with his arms folded and the tall steward with a little notebook",
        "👓 Lente da Realidade: Uma Vila no Meio do Campo": "👓 Reality Lens: A Town in the Middle of the Countryside",
        "✨ O Sonho de Sancho: Uma Ilha com Mar, Praia e Coqueiro!": "✨ Sancho’s Dream: An Island with a Sea, a Beach and a Palm Tree!",
        "Sancho conta os chapeuzinhos...": "Sancho counts the tiny caps...",
        "À mesa do banquete, o doutor magro toca um prato com a varinha e um criado o leva embora; Sancho, de guardanapo no pescoço, olha desesperado": "At the banquet table, the thin doctor touches a plate with his wand and a servant carries it away; Sancho, with his napkin round his neck, looks on in despair",
        "🍗 O Doutor que Não Deixava Comer": "🍗 The Doctor Who Wouldn’t Let Him Eat",
        "Sancho quer abrir o esconderijo das moedas. Toque no nome dele! Cuidado com as pegadinhas!": "Sancho wants to open the coins’ hiding place. Tap its name! Watch out for the trick words!",
        "No tribunal da vila, Sancho governador observa dois velhinhos: um jura com a mão no peito, enquanto o outro, um aldeão, segura a bengala grossa de madeira": "In the town courtroom, Governor Sancho watches two old men: one swears with his hand on his heart, while the other, a villager, holds the thick wooden cane",
        "👓 Lente da Realidade: O Tribunal da Vila": "👓 Reality Lens: The Town Courtroom",
        "✨ Lente da Imaginação: O Trono do Sábio Governador!": "✨ Imagination Lens: The Throne of the Wise Governor!",
        "🔎 O segredo está na:": "🔎 The secret is in the:",
        "No tribunal da vila, Sancho abre os braços como os pratos de uma balança, enquanto um viajante aponta um desenho da ponte com os guardas e a cadeia logo na entrada e o mordomo coça a cabeça com o lápis": "In the town courtroom, Sancho opens his arms like the pans of a pair of scales, while a traveller points to a drawing of the bridge with the guards and the jail right at the entrance, and the steward scratches his head with his pencil",
        "🌉 A Ponte da Pergunta Difícil": "🌉 The Bridge of the Difficult Question",
        "Na beira do riacho da aldeia, um pajem ajoelhado entrega a Teresa, de avental, uma carta e um colar de corais; Sanchica pula de alegria": "On the bank of the village stream, a kneeling page hands Teresa, in her apron, a letter and a coral necklace; Sanchica jumps for joy",
        "📿 Um Presente da Duquesa para Teresa": "📿 A Present from the Duchess for Teresa",
        "Ajude o governador a assinar a carta! Toque nas letras do primeiro nome dele, na ordem certa.": "Help the governor sign the letter! Tap the letters of his first name, in the right order.",
        "Na mesa do governador, Sancho segura a pena como um garfo diante da carta; o mordomo espera, de caderninho na mão": "At the governor’s desk, Sancho holds the quill like a fork in front of the letter; the steward waits, notebook in hand",
        "👓 Lente da Realidade: A Pena na Mão do Governador": "👓 Reality Lens: The Quill in the Governor’s Hand",
        "✨ O Sonho de Sancho: O Governador que Sabe Escrever!": "✨ Sancho’s Dream: The Governor Who Can Write!",
        "Assinado:": "Signed:",
        "No estábulo, Sancho, com a sua roupa simples, abraça o pescoço do burrico": "In the stable, Sancho, in his plain clothes, hugs the donkey round the neck",
        "🫏 Um Abraço no Velho Amigo": "🫏 A Hug for an Old Friend",
        "Dom Quixote, ao lado de Rocinante, olha para dentro de um buraco entre ruínas; lá no fundo, Sancho acena e o burrico zurra": "Don Quixote, beside Rocinante, looks down into a hole among some ruins; at the bottom, Sancho waves and the donkey brays",
        "🕳️ Uma Voz Conhecida no Fundo do Buraco": "🕳️ A Familiar Voice at the Bottom of the Hole",
        "Hora de deixar o palácio! Ajude os dois amigos a ir embora: toque nas palavras na ordem em que tudo acontece.": "Time to leave the palace! Help the two friends on their way: tap the words in the order that everything happens.",
        "No salão do palácio, Sancho, ajoelhado, devolve as chaves ao duque e à duquesa; depois o portão se abre, e os dois amigos seguem pela estrada aberta; bem longe, atrás deles, um cavaleiro sozinho com uma lua branca no escudo": "In the palace hall, Sancho, kneeling, gives the keys back to the duke and the duchess; then the gate opens, and the two friends set off along the open road; far behind them, a lone knight with a white moon on his shield",
        "👓 Lente da Realidade: Sancho Devolve a Ilha": "👓 Reality Lens: Sancho Gives Back the Island",
        "✨ Lente da Imaginação: A Estrada Dourada da Liberdade!": "✨ Imagination Lens: The Golden Road of Freedom!",
        "Hora de ir embora...": "Time to go...",
        "👓 Lente da Realidade: Dez Moedas Dentro da Bengala!": "👓 Reality Lens: Ten Coins Inside the Cane!",
        "👓 Lente da Realidade: As Chaves Voltam para o Duque": "👓 Reality Lens: The Keys Go Back to the Duke",
        "👓 Lente da Realidade: O Portão do Palácio se Abre!": "👓 Reality Lens: The Palace Gate Opens!",
        "🕊️ Lente da Realidade: De Volta à Estrada e ao Céu Aberto": "🕊️ Reality Lens: Back to the Road and the Open Sky",
        "🏝️ Fim da Parte 7! Os dois amigos estão juntos de novo, a caminho do mar. Mas quem é o cavaleiro da lua branca que vem atrás deles? A resposta está na última parte!": "🏝️ The End of Part 7! The two friends are together again, on their way to the sea. But who is the knight of the white moon coming along behind them? The answer is in the last part!",
        "Ler a Parte 8": "Read Part 8"
    }
};
